  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 309 PM EDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\nPrimary Forecast Concerns: \n\nShower/thunderstorm chances along a passing cold front Saturday: \nSurface cold front lying across northwest Ontario/northwest \nMinnesota/southeast North Dakota at early afternoon is forecast to \npropagate east through tonight...into the upper Great Lakes Saturday \nmorning...and across northern Michigan Saturday afternoon/evening. \nNarrow theta-e ridge runs ahead of the front...which will be the \nsource for low level moisture/instability for the front to work \nwith.  Not a lot of moisture in a relative sense (dry above 700mb) \nso raises some questions as to precipitation coverage being limited \nby dry air entrainment into updrafts that probably won't be all that \nrobust to begin with (there may be some low clouds (high based Sc) \nat the start of the day to inhibit heating/destabilization). Overall \na light precipitation event at best at this point...minimal \nprobabilities for rainfall exceeding 0.25 inch during the day \nSaturday.  Rain chances expected to wind down from west to east by \nSaturday evening. \n\nCooler with some small rain chances Sunday: Will be watching a short \nwave trough dig into the Lake Superior basin Sunday along with an \nassociated jet streak.  Can't rule out a few light showers as this \nfeature passes through mainly Sunday afternoon/evening but this also \nlooks pretty minimal.  After highs into the 70s and possibly some \nlower 80s Saturday...cold advection in the wake of Saturday's \nfrontal passage expected to drops highs down into the 60s (lower 70s \nin downslope areas adjacent to Saginaw Bay).  \n\nMore substantial rain event arrives Monday night/Tuesday; heavy\nrainfall potential: Monday itself is expected to be dry as a \nshort wave ridge and surface high pressure cross the Great \nLakes. In the wake of this ridging deep layer southwest flow \ndevelops Monday night...with a strong low level jet ahead of a \ncold front that will make slow inroads into the upper Lakes as \nit parallels the flow aloft. Thermal/moisture advection ahead of\nthis front expected to bring rain and some embedded \nthunderstorms back into northern Michigan Monday night and into \nTuesday...though may see a drying trend Tuesday afternoon \nespecially the farther north you go. Given the deep layer \nunidirectional flow QPF potential will be much greater than \nanything that falls this weekend: 1 inch/24h rainfall \nprobabilities (ending 0000Z Wednesday exceed 50 percent north of\nthe M-72 corridor...with probabilities around 25 percent of \nexceeding 2 inches/24 hours across eastern Upper. Thus the \nslight Excessive Rainfall Risk across Upper Michigan for Monday \nnight. Things to watch for beyond Tuesday will be how far front \nthe cold front can sink...if it lingers closer to the US-10 \ncorridor will be facing a more substantial heavy rain threat \nacross the southern portion of the forecast area.",
